You can say it, but it's the same as saying "ain't no".

Here's the deal.  Only "persons" can possess things in 
English.  If your dog is a person, you can talk about
your dog's bowl.  And here "possess" takes on every 
meaning from "Bill's money" to "Bill's wife" to
"Bill's country" to "Bill's God".  

Inanimate objects do not possess things. We don't
(properly) say "the car's door" but rather "the door
of the car".  If you're writing a story about a
car that comes to life, then of course it (she?)
may possess things.  She is now a person.

Exceptions to this rule is are 1. time and 2 money.
We can say "A nickel's worth of candy" or "A year's
interest."  

Now then, "his" and "hers" are possessive pronouns. 
You can use them as nouns or as adjectives, which is
why they sound OK either with or without the noun:

"That charm is Bill's."

"That charm is Bill's Amulet of Yendor."

"That charm is his."

"That charm is his Amulet of Yendor."


Both possessives work both ways.  But "its" is only
an adjective and can't stand alone.  It must have the
noun to go with it.  The word can show a possessive link,
but it is not really a possessive in the grammatical 
sense.  It's not really a pronoun, even though it
looks an awefully lot like one.

(I'm not the grammar police, I'm just reporting what
"proper usage" is.  I could care less whether anyone
actually uses it. )

